vergence behavior.", "validation_plan_suggested_by_judge": "After applying the sanitizer, run the next iteration and confirm all quality_score outputs are within [0,1] (max \u2264 1.0, min \u2265 0.0). Check that the mean drops from ~1.8938 into the valid range and re-evaluate best/worst and std to determine if tight clustering persists or was an artifact of mis-scaling.", "self_reported_confidence_uncalibrated": 0.8, "target_assessment": null}, "notes": [], "judge_raw_output": "The analyzer shows a clear mismatch between the documented range of quality_score ([0,1]) and the observed values (mean = 1.8938, best = 1.9000, std = 0.0135). This points to an implementation-level scoring/output problem. A minimal, targeted fix is to sanitize the quality_score outputs to the documented range so the optimizer sees consistent inputs before making other changes.\n\n<decision>{\n  \"failure_location\": \"implementation\",\n  \"root_cause_hypothesis\": \"The quality_score scorer is producing outputs outside its documented [0,1] range (observed mean=1.8938, best=1.9000, std=0.0135), likely due to a scaling/aggregation or post-processing bug in the scorer or reporting pipeline.\",\n  \"intervention_target\": \"implementation\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Observed quality_score values (mean 1.8938, best 1.9000) violate the documented [0,1] range and show extremely low variance (std 0.0135). Sanitizing outputs to [0,1] is a minimal, reversible change that enforces the intended numerical contract and prevents optimizer decisions being driven by mis-scaled values.\",\n  \"proposed_change\": {\n    \"objective_name\": \"quality_score\",\n    \"action\": \"sanitize_outputs\",\n    \"valid_range\": [0.0, 1.0]\n  },\n  \"expected_benefit\": \"Restore the intended numerical range for quality_score so objective weighting and comparisons are meaningful. This should eliminate misinterpretation of progress, allow correct trade-offs with parameter_spread, and reveal true variance/convergence behavior